#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "9cfff4db-e33e-5a3c-a1d4-99e910f2c2fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-10897" :question/problem "Simplify the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx .\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx = 2 \\int_{0}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "a3e0d847-4dd1-5a47-948c-06959065b73f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10898" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =4(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156928 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "29ca6f83-ceb9-54fa-b8c4-6c0fbbd4b742" :question/math-academy-id "e-10911" :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A,$ what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7693e9fc-715d-5d4d-8002-ce63e512ffd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291815} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc11f47-51f1-5a8c-851f-3d243ecac4c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291816} {:answer/id #uuid "8e068557-ac8e-588f-a9b7-df6e0c9cc732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291817} {:answer/id #uuid "8c583a2e-1c81-531d-8430-3b23943e4231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291818} {:answer/id #uuid "f2baff23-fbf6-5a97-ad1e-e555e37302b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7693e9fc-715d-5d4d-8002-ce63e512ffd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291815} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3aeaeec-0dd5-5acd-a556-85aa12c46f76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01023191-e11f-5c48-bf20-f38a6d28c5ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-115259" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-x)^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504ba205-c349-541c-b744-4c22e4694fcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291850} {:answer/id #uuid "cc917853-2cff-50ea-b159-bcf4beeaf0dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291851} {:answer/id #uuid "6b059072-2929-511d-ae52-4872fa0d647c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291852} {:answer/id #uuid "00023582-f48e-51ee-94c5-189c2664b183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291853} {:answer/id #uuid "9d256e7f-9c96-5c13-beb9-4be28f2c02c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b059072-2929-511d-ae52-4872fa0d647c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291852} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e12088-410d-56ff-8b61-0d283d8401f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8db7c7d-2c36-54ae-a136-cfed51754274" :question/math-academy-id "q-88208" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-x)^{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x+1) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-x) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+2x \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f216eece-2660-5e20-9d7a-870c2f0e3afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291864} {:answer/id #uuid "3baddf13-f0b6-5ace-9873-99dc05236d87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291865} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4e5687-aa81-58b3-aa6c-3b379ddfe892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291866} {:answer/id #uuid "b526c854-3d10-5f5e-9ef9-67e95870a066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291867} {:answer/id #uuid "26faacf8-1d22-5b0d-8a19-66e6dba74e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f216eece-2660-5e20-9d7a-870c2f0e3afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291864} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb72f35-3021-589c-bada-e1dd8e941a1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e5fda12-e16b-50c6-8bc6-86826ee8106a" :question/math-academy-id "q-88585" :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡((-x)^{4}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}]}
